Matches:

/journal/anything
Chapter 52: HTTP Basic Authentication Configuration

The instructor switches from:

.formLogin()

to:

.httpBasic()

Reason:

Application is API-based.

Not browser-based.

Frontend will send:

Authorization Header

instead of displaying login forms.

Therefore:

http.httpBasic();

is more appropriate.

Chapter 53: Public and Secured APIs

Instructor separates endpoints.

Public APIs

Accessible without authentication.

Examples:

/public/**

Used for:

Registration
Health Check
Signup
Secured APIs

Require authentication.

Examples:

/user/**
/journal/**

Used for:

Update User
Delete User
View Journals
Chapter 54: CSRF (Cross Site Request Forgery)

The instructor introduces:

CSRF
Definition

Cross Site Request Forgery.

A security attack where a user is tricked into sending an unwanted authenticated request.

Bank Example

Instructor's example:

Suppose user is logged into:

Bank Website

Browser stores:

Session Cookie

User then visits:

Malicious Website

Malicious website contains:

<button>
Click Here
</button>

User clicks button.

Button secretly triggers:

POST /transferMoney

Because browser already contains bank session cookie:

Bank believes:

Request came from authenticated user

Money gets transferred.

Why Attack Works

Because browser automatically sends:

Session Cookie

to bank.

Bank trusts session.

Chapter 55: CSRF Protection

To prevent attack:

Spring Security uses:

CSRF Token
How CSRF Token Works

Valid webpage contains:

<input hidden
value="csrfToken">

Request must contain:

Valid CSRF Token

If missing:

Request rejected.

Response:

403 Forbidden
Why Attacker Cannot Generate Token

Because:

CSRF token is generated by legitimate server.

Attacker does not know it.

Therefore:

Attack fails.

Chapter 56: Why CSRF Is Disabled Here

Instructor disables CSRF.

http.csrf().disable();

Reason:

Application is:

REST API

Using:

HTTP Basic Authentication

Not relying on browser sessions.

No HTML forms.

No session cookies.

Therefore:

CSRF protection unnecessary.

Chapter 57: Session Management

Instructor notes:

Spring Security sometimes mixes:

HTTP Basic Authentication

with

Session Management

This can create stateful behavior.

But API should remain:

Stateless
Stateless Configuration
http
.sessionManagement()
.sessionCreationPolicy(
SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS
);
Meaning

Do NOT create sessions.

Do NOT create JSESSIONID.

Do NOT store authentication state.

Every request must authenticate itself.

Benefits
Simpler

Every request independent.

Scalable

No session storage needed.

Better for APIs

Works well with:

Mobile Apps
React Frontend
Angular Frontend
Microservices
Chapter 58: Problem with Path Parameters

Old approach:

GET /journal/ram

Problem:

Username manually supplied.

User can replace:

ram

with:

shyam

Potential security issue.

Better Approach

Use authenticated user.

Spring Security already knows:

Who logged in

No need to pass username.

Chapter 59: SecurityContextHolder

Instructor introduces:

SecurityContextHolder
Purpose

Stores authentication information of current authenticated user.

Getting Authentication Object
Authentication auth =
SecurityContextHolder
.getContext()
.getAuthentication();
Getting Username
auth.getName();

Returns:

ram

if Ram logged in.

Why This Is Better

Instead of:

GET /journal/ram

use:

auth.getName();

Now user cannot access another user's data.

Chapter 60: Update User API

Instructor creates:

PUT /user

This endpoint is secured.

Authentication required.

Flow

Step 1

User logs in.

Step 2

Spring Security authenticates.

Step 3

Username retrieved:

auth.getName();

Step 4

Fetch user from MongoDB.

Step 5

Update fields.

Step 6

Hash new password.

Step 7

Save user.

Password Update Example

Old Password:

ram

New Password:

abc

Before saving:

passwordEncoder.encode("abc");

Store hash.

Never store plain text.

Chapter 61: Delete User API

Instructor creates:

DELETE /user

No username parameter required.

Use:

auth.getName();

Then:

userRepository
.deleteByUserName(
auth.getName()
);

Result:

Authenticated user deletes own account.

Cannot delete someone else's account.

Final Secure Architecture
Public APIs
/public/create-user

Accessible by everyone.

Authenticated APIs
/user/**
/journal/**

Require login.

Authentication Source
MongoDB
Password Storage
BCrypt Hash
Session Policy
STATELESS
Authentication Mechanism
HTTP Basic Authentication
Current User Access
SecurityContextHolder
Complete Flow of the Application
User Registers
↓
Password Hashed
↓
Saved to MongoDB
↓
User Sends Username + Password
↓
HTTP Basic Authentication
↓
CustomUserDetailsService
↓
MongoDB User Fetch
↓
BCrypt Verification
↓
Authentication Success
↓
SecurityContextHolder Stores User
↓
Journal/User APIs Accessed
↓
auth.getName() Returns Logged-In User
↓
User Sees Only Own Data